      "skill": "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill",
      "language": "en",
      "prompt": "Request B: A separate verified fictional interface learns two condition-specific encoder/classifier branches from their respective labelled source datasets and aligns their features using a permitted unlabeled target training pool. At prediction, the same single target window is processed by both frozen branches; their class-probability vectors are averaged for the diagnosis. No source training samples, second sensor modality, additional feature fusion or parameter updates are used at prediction. The classes are the same across these datasets; this is the supplied task, not a universal adaptation requirement. Write three positive reusable English prose blocks and proportional author/review notes: (1) a 40-70-word abstract/method chain linking training populations, target-guided learning and the actual prediction combination; (2) a 20-40-word candidate contribution connecting condition variation to that learning/decision interface without claiming routine adaptation is novel; (3) a 20-35-word data/evidence description preserving Case D's counts without calling views new acquisitions or claiming industrial performance. Distinguish features, logits and probabilities rather than silently changing the stated probability average. Retain the allowed unlabeled target access and put unspecified selection/design/novelty in notes. Do not assert cross-site generalization, physical causality or gains not supplied, or require every paper to adopt these branches. State word/sentence counts. These are fictional classification/counting/prose fixtures, not research experiments.",
